---
/**
 * Forms guide.
 *
 * The behavior documented here is owned by the web components and proven by
 * the test files named inline; every claim below is copied from that code and
 * those comments, plus the FACE contract on `form-control-base.ts`.
 */
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro"
---

<Layout title="Forms" description="How Santaka UI form controls participate in a plain HTML form." sidebar>
  <h1>Forms</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A form control in this system participates in a plain <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>. The framework
    adapter must not be what makes submission work: if a form only submits when React, Vue, or a
    helper library collects the values, the control is broken.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Name and value</h2>
    <p>
      The platform is what submits, so the platform's rules are the contract. <code>name</code> is
      what the form looks up; <code>value</code> is what arrives. A form-associated custom element
      with no <code>name</code> is unnamed, and an unnamed control contributes nothing to
      <code>FormData</code> — it is not submitted empty, it is not submitted at all.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not a hypothetical failure. Three of the React adapters (<code>Checkbox</code>,
      <code>Switch</code>, <code>Select</code>) dropped <code>name</code> in an earlier revision.
      The failure was invisible from inside React: the field rendered, took focus, validated, and
      reported its value through <code>onValueChange</code>, and the form submitted without it. The
      adapters' own tests run under jsdom, which has no form submission and no form-associated
      custom element implementation, so nothing looked at <code>name</code> and nothing caught it.
      An empty <code>name</code> is not a safe fallback either: the platform treats
      <code>name=""</code> as a name and submits the control under it, so the adapters omit the
      attribute entirely when no name is given.
    </p>
    <p>
      The regression story and the fix are documented in
      <code>packages/react/src/form-name.test.tsx</code>. That file is the jsdom half — it asserts
      the adapters put <code>name</code> on the element. The browser half, where the claim is real,
      is <code>examples/react-vite/e2e/form-submission.e2e.ts</code>: it renders every control
      through <code>@santaka-ui/react</code>, submits a plain <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>, and reads
      the entries from the browser's own <code>FormData</code>, not from React state.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Validation</h2>
    <p>
      <code>required</code>, <code>minlength</code>, <code>maxlength</code>, and
      <code>pattern</code> become real attributes on the inner native control, so the browser does
      the checking, the error bubble appears in the browser's own language, and <code>:invalid</code>
      styling works without JavaScript. Constraint validation is never suppressed by the
      component.
    </p>
    <p>
      The consumer still owns the cases native validation cannot express — cross-field checks,
      async checks — through two independent props. <code>invalid</code> marks the field invalid
      before the reason is known; <code>error</code> carries the message. They are separate on
      purpose: a field can be invalid before the consumer knows why, and an error message can be
      present without the field being in an error state, such as a form-level summary listing
      several fields. A field is invalid from either signal, and <code>error</code>'s presence
      drives <code>aria-invalid</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      What the component does not do is block submission: the browser blocks submission on a
      <code>required</code> field; the component does not, and React does not — that split is
      asserted in <code>examples/react-vite/e2e/form-submission.e2e.ts</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The FACE callbacks</h2>
    <p>
      <code>sk-input</code>, <code>sk-textarea</code>, and <code>sk-checkbox</code> are
      form-associated custom elements: they call <code>attachInternals()</code>, push value and
      validity with <code>setFormValue()</code>, and implement three callbacks.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>formResetCallback()</code> — reset restores the initial value, the one the adapter
        rendered, not the last typed one.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>formDisabledCallback(disabled)</code> — reflects a disabled ancestor
        <code>fieldset</code>. A disabled control withholds its field from submission, which is why
        the submission evidence tests name the paths that are withheld rather than submitted
        empty.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>formStateRestoreCallback(state, mode)</code> — restores values after a browser
        session restore or autocomplete refill.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>The bfcache caveat, exactly as the test file documents it:</strong> the obvious
      candidate trigger, the back/forward cache, does not call the callback. Measured in Chromium,
      navigating away and calling <code>page.goBack()</code> restores the DOM intact, so the value
      survives without <code>formStateRestoreCallback</code> running once — a test built on that
      navigation would pass with the callback deleted. No session restore or autocomplete refill is
      drivable from a page test either. So
      <code>packages/web-components/e2e/form-state-restore.e2e.ts</code> invokes the callback
      directly — <code>el.formStateRestoreCallback(state, "restore")</code> — the exact call the
      platform makes, with the exact arguments. What that suite does <em>not</em> prove is that the
      platform calls it; that is the browser's contract, not this library's. What it proves is the
      part that is this library's: that when the call arrives, the value lands in the prop, in the
      rendered control, and in <code>FormData</code>; that it fires no <code>sk-value-change</code>
      (a restore is not a user action); and that a mode other than <code>"restore"</code> is
      ignored.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The checkbox is a boolean, not a string</h2>
    <p>
      The shared text base speaks strings, so <code>sk-checkbox</code> owns its own boolean path
      while reusing the field wiring. The native <code>change</code> event carries the same
      <code>value</code> attribute whether the box was just checked or unchecked; translating at
      the accessor boundary was the first attempt and got unchecking wrong — every toggle read back
      as checked. So the component maps the boolean itself: a checked box submits its
      <code>value</code> (<code>"on"</code> by default, the platform default), an unchecked box is
      withheld entirely rather than submitted empty.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>indeterminate</code> is visual state, not form state. It is property-only on the
      platform — there is no attribute — so the prop is written to the DOM node after each render.
      An indeterminate checkbox submits as unchecked, which is what the platform does: a
      half-answer is not an answer.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Using the controls in React</h2>
    <p>
      The adapter's channel for value changes is <code>onValueChange</code>, not
      <code>onChange</code>. The element fires <code>sk-value-change</code>
      <strong>only for user-initiated changes</strong>, never for a programmatic update, and the
      adapter forwards that event's detail: a string for <code>Input</code>,
      <code>Textarea</code>, <code>Select</code>, and <code>RadioGroup</code>; a boolean for
      <code>Switch</code>'s <code>checked</code>. The event carries a <code>CustomEvent</code>
      detail, which is what makes <code>onValueChange=&#123;(value) => &#123;…&#125;&#125;</code>
      typed rather than an <code>onChange</code> passthrough.
    </p>
    <p>
      Name and submitted value stay consumer-owned through ordinary props: <code>name</code>
      reaches the element as an attribute (omitted, never empty, when unspecified), and
      <code>value</code> chooses what the form receives — the checkbox test asserts
      <code>value="accepted"</code> reads back as a property with the default still
      <code>"on"</code>, matching a native checkbox.
    </p>
    <pre><code>import &#123; Input &#125; from "@santaka-ui/react"

&#60;form name="signup"&#62;
  &#60;Input
    name="email"
    type="email"
    required
    label="Email"
    error=&#123;errors.email&#125;
    onValueChange=&#123;(value) =&#62; setEmail(value)&#125;
  /&#62;
&#60;/form&#62;</code></pre>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What is proven where</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>packages/react/src/form-name.test.tsx</code> — the adapters forward <code>name</code>
        and omit it entirely when unspecified; that file is where the regression is caught.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>examples/react-vite/e2e/form-submission.e2e.ts</code> — every control submits through
        the browser's own <code>FormData</code>; unchecked/off controls are withheld; the browser
        blocks on <code>required</code>; reset restores initial values; values reach
        <code>FormData</code>, not React state.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>packages/web-components/e2e/form-state-restore.e2e.ts</code> —
        <code>formStateRestoreCallback</code> for all six form-associated controls, including the
        bfcache caveat and the non-<code>"restore"</code> mode being ignored.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .lede {
    max-inline-size: 65ch;
  }
  pre {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding: 0.75rem 1rem;
    border: var(--docs-stroke) solid var(--docs-edge);
  }
</style>
